# Environment for the Forgejo MCP server. See quadlets/forgejomcp.container.
#
# This file is where the unit's exposure is decided, not the network: the port is
# published on loopback, and what keeps that safe is the fallback being off.

FORGEJO_URL=(url of your Forgejo instance, e.g. https://forgejo.example.org)
FORGEJO_ACCESS_TOKEN=(create one in Forgejo under Settings -> Applications -> Access Tokens)

# Host names the server answers to, matched exactly with no wildcard; a name
# missing here is a 403 on every request. The template covers the container's own
# name on devnetng and the loopback families. A client on another machine sends
# its own Host -- over a LAN address that is "<host>:8080", and that string
# belongs here too. An SSH tunnel keeps the client on 127.0.0.1 and needs no
# change. The server logs the Host it refused, so a 403 names its own fix.
FORGEJO_MCP_ALLOWED_HOSTS=systemd-forgejomcp,localhost,127.0.0.1,[::1]

# OFF, and this is the whole reason the port above may be published. With it on,
# a request that carries no Authorization header is served with
# FORGEJO_ACCESS_TOKEN, so anything that can reach the port acts as the
# operator's Forgejo user without presenting a credential -- fine inside a
# network only containers can join, not fine on a published port.
#
# Off means every request carries its own token, which is also the better model
# for several development environments: each one uses its own Forgejo identity,
# and revoking one does not disturb the others. FORGEJO_ACCESS_TOKEN stays in
# this file because the server wants one at startup, but with the fallback off no
# request is served with it.
FORGEJO_MCP_ALLOW_OPERATOR_TOKEN_FALLBACK=0
